// FastDRC constraint-formula grammar (rawast).
//
// The surface syntax is a strict subset of Python expression syntax, so every
// constraint is also valid Python and `ast.parse(expr, mode="eval")` can serve
// as a differential oracle in tests.
//
// A constraint is a boolean combination of atomic scalar relations. It
// expresses only what the drawing cannot show: layer/net assignment and
// numeric/angular relations between an entity's scalar attributes and
// declared measures. Spatial topology (inside, overlap, enclosure, abutment,
// coincidence, touch) is DERIVED from the drawn geometry -- never typed here.
//
// A `qualified_identifier` resolves in the semantic layer to one of:
//   - a declared measure variable        (X)
//   - an entity attribute                 (A.layer, A.net, A.width, e2.angle)
//   - a bare layer / enum literal         (metal5)
//
// Tiers, low -> high precedence (Python's):
//   or  <  and  <  not  <  (cmp | in)  <  + -  <  * /  <  primary
//
// The grammar emits the FINAL {op, args[]} AST directly -- no post-parse
// lowering. Every operator level is `choice { <MULTI/BIN/CMP>, <NEXT> }`: the
// operator form is tried first and, on a missing operator, PEG's ordered choice
// falls back to <NEXT>, so a single operand passes straight through with no
// wrapper. Single-operator tiers (or/and) emit a flat n-ary node; cmp / +-/ */
// emit binary; `not` emits unary -- all uniform `{op, args:[...]}`. Leaves are
// {ref:str} / {num:int|float} / {set:[node,...]}.
//
// Requires rawast >= 0.1.1 (single-quote 'keyword' = strict word-bounded Key,
// so 'not'/'and'/'or'/'in' don't bite identifier prefixes like `notch`).
//
// NOTES:
//  - PEG can't left-fold, so a chain of 3+ math operands at one level needs
//    parens: `a - b` is binary, but `a - b - c` is a syntax error -- write
//    `(a - b) - c`. (Comparisons and boolean tiers are unaffected.)
//  - The shared-first-token choices below are flagged by `rawast lint` as
//    predictive-ambiguous; they are correct via PEG ordered-choice fallback.
//    Set `backtrack: true` on those choices to silence the warning.

use: std

start: <OR>

// or/and are single-operator levels: a `repeat+2 ... separator` list of operands
// (≥2, so a lone operand fails the MULTI form and the choice falls through to
// <NEXT> with no wrapper). The op is bound on the separator token. Flat n-ary.
OR ignore whitespace line_comment block_comment: choice { <OR_MULTI>, <AND> }
OR_MULTI: sequence dict { repeat+2 <AND>:args[]=@ separator 'or':op="or" }

AND: choice { <AND_MULTI>, <NOT> }
AND_MULTI: sequence dict { repeat+2 <NOT>:args[]=@ separator 'and':op="and" }

NOT: choice { <NEG>, <REL> }
NEG: sequence dict { 'not':op="not", <NOT>:args[]=@ }

// One atomic relation: a comparison, or a bare arithmetic expression.
REL: choice { <CMP>, <ADD> }
CMP: sequence dict { <ADD>:args[]=@, <CMP_OP>:op=@, <CMP_RHS>:args[]=@ }
// Longest-first so "<=" wins over "<" and ">=" over ">". 'in' is a strict keyword.
CMP_OP: choice { "==":@, "!=":@, "<=":@, ">=":@, "<":@, ">":@, 'in':@ }
CMP_RHS: choice { <SET>, <ADD> }

// Arithmetic. Binary only (PEG can't left-fold); parenthesize 3+ chains.
ADD: choice { <ADD_BIN>, <MUL> }
ADD_BIN: sequence dict { <MUL>:args[]=@, <ADD_OP>:op=@, <MUL>:args[]=@ }
ADD_OP: choice { "+":@, "-":@ }

MUL: choice { <MUL_BIN>, <PRIMARY> }
MUL_BIN: sequence dict { <PRIMARY>:args[]=@, <MUL_OP>:op=@, <PRIMARY>:args[]=@ }
MUL_OP: choice { "*":@, "/":@ }

PRIMARY: choice { <GROUP>, <NUMBER>, <COUNT>, <AREA>, <REF> }
// The one function: Count(K) -- how many of K's layer a shape holds (which shape is
// read from the drawing: the one K is drawn in). Tried before <REF> so that the
// name is not taken for an identifier.
COUNT: sequence dict { <COUNT_KW>:op="count", "(", <REF>:args[]=@, ")" }
COUNT_KW: choice { 'Count', 'count' }
// Area(K) -- how much area of K's layer that shape holds, all of it together.
AREA: sequence dict { <AREA_KW>:op="area", "(", <REF>:args[]=@, ")" }
AREA_KW: choice { 'Area', 'area' }
GROUP: sequence { "(", <OR>, ")" }
// `int`/`float` are signed parsers, so -45 parses natively as {num:-45}.
// `float` precedes `int` (strict float fails on a bare integer, letting int win).
NUMBER: sequence dict { <NUMLIT>:num=@ }
NUMLIT: choice { float, int }
REF: sequence dict { qualified_identifier:ref=@ }

// Set literal -- valid only as the right-hand side of `in`.
SET: sequence dict { "{", <SET_ELEMS>:set=@, "}" }
SET_ELEMS: sequence array { repeat <REF> separator "," }
